  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "JjwHMw+MiGqL2LzYfVdodMXASoo=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:43:43.664Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "`@renown/sdk` is the core authentication SDK \u2014 credential verification, session management, cryptographic operations \u2014 and it is constructed through a builder:\n\n```ts\nconst renown = await new RenownBuilder(\"my-app\")\n  .withBaseUrl(\"https://www.renown.id\")\n  .build();\n```\n\nEvery dependency is injectable, which is what makes the SDK testable and re-hostable:\n\n- `withBaseUrl(url)` \u2014 the Renown server, default `https://www.renown.id`;\n- `withStorage(storage)` \u2014 user-data persistence, **default in-memory** (so a host that wants a session to survive a reload must supply one);\n- `withEventEmitter(emitter)` \u2014 for state changes;\n- `withCrypto(crypto)` \u2014 a pre-built crypto instance;\n- `withKeyPairStorage(storage)` \u2014 key-pair storage, **from which crypto is built** if no instance is given;\n- `withProfileFetcher(fetcher)` \u2014 a custom profile-enrichment strategy.\n\nThe division of labour with React is stated explicitly in `COMPONENTS.md`: **React components and hooks for Renown are provided by `@powerhousedao/reactor-browser`, not by `@renown/sdk` directly** \u2014 initialised with either a `<Renown appName onError>` component or the `useRenownInit` hook. So a Node or CLI consumer takes the SDK alone, and a React host takes reactor-browser, which wraps it.\n\n## Provenance\n- `packages/renown/README.md` \u2014 intro; RenownBuilder and builder methods\n- `packages/renown/COMPONENTS.md` \u2014 intro (components live in reactor-browser); Setup\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T07:43:43.664Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:43:43.664Z"
    }
  },
